\f1\fs22 \'93Hello, Sarah.\'94 A whisper from within the hooded robe of a medieval monk.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You!\'94 Sarah sat up, ready for conflict, by word or deed, or both. The snap from sleeping to shock gave her head a crack. Her heart kicked against her ribs\'97some primal feminine alarm she resented immediately.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Me,\'94 Kleoboulos Windus, allowed. \'93Please don\'92t scream.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah call for help? The presumption that she might was absurd. \'93How did you get in here? How did you even get 
\f2\i here
\f1\i0 ?\'94 Eloise would be incensed to learn her security was so lax. And Eloise 
\f2\i would
\f1\i0  learn her security was so lax.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo shrugged. \'93I have a gift of infiltrating places unnoticed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt a threat in his confidence. And something about the light in the room made Kleo appear more apparition than physical, somewhere between grandad flesh and ancient poltergeist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo guessed her thoughts. \'93What you sense as a quality of otherworldliness is a product of my weariness.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked Kleo up and down. \'93You can\'92t hurt me, old man.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo smiled and tented his fingers together. \'93Feeling invincible is not the same as being invincible. Catastrophic trauma would end you as easily as any other\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Are you here to kill me?\'94 Sarah taunted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am here to offer you life, and my service.\'94 Kleo took two gliding steps towards Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve made my life Hell!\'94 she hissed, aware of Kathy on the couch near the balcony doors, double-patched and finally sleeping after an hour\'92s complaint about all the nature outside the window. She could complicate an already unpredictable circumstance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am truly sorry for the way you became pregnant. It was wrong. I wanted, needed your consent. Others thought your enrolment surplus to requirements.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Convenient. Just blame some other lunatic in your cult.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo appeared genuinely wounded by Sarah\'92s words. Sensing an advantage, she pressed. \'93Tell me the truth. Did Tony have any part in getting me pregnant? Any part at all?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo shook his head. \'93No. He has his part to play in coming events, but as to your pregnancy, he\'92s blameless. Ignorant, to be more accurate, and therefore as innocent as a newborn lamb.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She felt her eyes sting, then the embarrassment of actual tears\'97her own, apparently\'97running down her face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo stepped closer. Sarah should have pulled away, should have maintained distance from this man who\'92d violated her. But something shifted in her belly\'97Little Clinton moving, or perhaps just the weight of him\'97and the sensation pinned her in place. Kleo reached up and cradled her cheek with surprising gentleness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, young Sarah, Tony Chantara is not lost to you. There are wheels within wheels, and he will cycle back around many times yet before this is all done.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony\'92s coming back to me on a bicycle,\'94 Sarah cracked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo entertained the vision. \'93Probably more of a monocycle,\'94 he answered, and smiled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt the weight of everything collapse inward. She recoiled from Kleo\'92s touch and sat back against the bed head for support. She wiped moisture from her face. \'93Why does it have to be my baby?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re special, young Sarah,\'94 Kleo whispered, a raspy voice of empathy and compassion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grimaced at being called young. She studied Kleo\'92s face. \'93You know? You know how I\'92m special.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a pause, Kleo admitted, \'93I do.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All part of the prophecy, I suppose.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even at a whisper, Kleo could hear her bitterness. \'93Not exactly, but certainly a blessing for furthering it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you know how I became special?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sage nodded. \'93I believe so.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah set her jaw and dared Kleo continue.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There are better times ahead for revealing such things,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded, assessing, wanting Kleo to be bluffing. At the heart of all cultish craft was the art of the bluff. \'93Leave,\'94 she told him. She watched his feet, waiting to see which way they\'92d turn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am here to give you comfort and succour.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re doing a taxing shit job of it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Young Sarah. I know how invested you are in your ability to rise above all obstacles.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I can look after myself.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo wobbled his head. \'93Mostly. Until you can\'92t.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So taking me down a peg is your way of giving me comfort and succour?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93In a way, yes. Your\'85 talents\'85 have carried you this far. But now it\'92s time to accept assistance from any quarter.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah let silence expand while she tried to read Kleo\'92s thoughts. \'93You mean Eloise.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo nodded. \'93And others.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re telling me to marry Alexander.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is consistent with the prophecy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, well if it\'92s 
\f2\i consistent
\f1\i0 \'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The marriage will be a public shell only.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How would you know?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo pressed his lips together, considering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s consistent with the prophecy,\'94 Sarah said for him. \'93Not quite a holy birth, is it? Not exactly virgin.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93More extra-virgin, I suppose.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah choked back laughter, not wanting to wake Kathy. \'93You think I\'92ve never fucked anyone?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo let the silence stretch, then: \'93Talk it out with Eloise. Do what you do so well: gather the facts, weigh the options.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What is so important about my baby anyway?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What matters is what the child represents. A different way of seeing things\'97less superstition, more\'85\'94 He searched for the right word. \'93Clarity.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s eyes moved to the window. Eloise\'92s compound occupied the dark beyond the glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And greed?\'94 Sarah found herself asking. It surprised her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ultimately, it\'92s always about greed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what of the Elvi?\'94 she asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think you can see they are faring rather well.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Will that continue?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You will see to it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And that\'92s part of the prophecy, is it?\'94 Sarah asked sceptically.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not so much,\'94 Kleo conceded. \'93It\'92s a laudable by-product of you being you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah started tearing up again. Taxing Elvi\'97why do I care so much about them?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Everyone will be better off.\'94 Kleo pressed his fingertips together and rocked his hands gently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded. The certainty in his voice steadied something in her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah set her jaw. \'93I\'92m still not letting you off for the evil shit you did to me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The burden of your scorn is a weight I am willing to bear.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tears were coming faster now, hot and unwelcome, clogging her throat and blurring her vision. She turned and raced for the bed, pressing her face into the sheet to muffle any sound. She blinked hard until her sight cleared.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo was gone. Sarah wasn\'92t sure she\'92d heard the door open or close.
